## PoniaEmu @VERSION@

A game launcher with a built-in Libretro player (PoniaPlay). Play 20+ retro systems without RetroArch.
Based on Pegasus Frontend, ported to Qt 6. See the [README](https://github.com/ponia2026/PoniaEmu#readme)
for the supported systems, setup and build instructions.

### Downloads

| File | For |
| --- | --- |
| `PoniaEmu_@TAG@-@STAMP@_v8a.apk` | Android, 64-bit (arm64-v8a), Android 6.0 or newer |
| `PoniaEmu_@TAG@-@STAMP@_v7a.apk` | Android, 32-bit (armeabi-v7a), Android 6.0 or newer |
| `PoniaEmu_@TAG@-@STAMP@_linux-x86_64.tar.gz` | Linux, 64-bit PC |
| `PoniaEmu_@TAG@-@STAMP@_linux-arm64.tar.gz` | Linux, ARM 64-bit |
| `PoniaEmu_@TAG@-@STAMP@_linux-armhf.tar.gz` | Linux, ARM 32-bit |
| `SHA256SUMS.txt` | Checksums of all files above |

### Install on Android (directly on the device, no PC, no root, no adb)

A phone, tablet or TV box can install the APK by itself:

1. On the device, open this page in its web browser and download the `.apk` file that fits it (see below for how to choose).
   Or download it on a PC and copy it to the device with a USB stick, then open it from the device's file manager.
2. Open the downloaded file. The first time, Android asks to allow installing apps from this source
   (the browser or the file manager): allow it, go back and open the file again.
3. Tap **Install**, then **Open**. If Play Protect warns that the app is unknown, choose **Install anyway**:
   the APK is not distributed through Google Play. Check `SHA256SUMS.txt` if you want to verify the download.

Which file? Almost every phone and TV box made since 2017 is 64-bit: use `_v8a`. Older or low-cost devices can be
32-bit: use `_v7a`. If unsure, try `_v8a` first; a 32-bit device refuses it with "App not installed" and nothing is changed.
You can also find out in **Settings → About** (a "64-bit" or "arm64" mention), or with the adb command below.

To update, install the newer APK over the old one (settings and saves are kept). If Android refuses it, an earlier test
build signed with a different key is installed: uninstall PoniaEmu first, which also deletes its settings.

### Install on Android (from a PC, with adb)

Not sure which APK you need? Ask the device:

```sh
adb shell getprop ro.product.cpu.abilist
```

If the list contains `arm64-v8a`, use the `_v8a` file. If it only has `armeabi-v7a`, use the `_v7a` file.
Over the network, connect first with `adb connect <device-ip>:5555`.

64-bit:

```sh
curl -fLO https://github.com/ponia2026/PoniaEmu/releases/download/@TAG@/PoniaEmu_@TAG@-@STAMP@_v8a.apk
adb install -r PoniaEmu_@TAG@-@STAMP@_v8a.apk
```

32-bit:

```sh
curl -fLO https://github.com/ponia2026/PoniaEmu/releases/download/@TAG@/PoniaEmu_@TAG@-@STAMP@_v7a.apk
adb install -r PoniaEmu_@TAG@-@STAMP@_v7a.apk
```

On Windows PowerShell, write `curl.exe` instead of `curl`.
If `adb install` complains that the signature does not match, an earlier test build signed with a different key is
installed. Remove it first with `adb uninstall org.ponia.PoniaEmu` (this also deletes its settings), then install again.

### Install on Linux (one command)

```sh
curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ponia2026/PoniaEmu/qt6/etc/linux/install.sh | sh
```

The script picks the right package for your system (x86_64, arm64 or armhf), checks its SHA-256, installs the Qt 6
runtime packages with apt and unpacks PoniaEmu into `/usr/local`. Start it with `poniaemu`.

On a box **without a desktop** (a TV box or a small board) the script also:

- installs a sound server (PulseAudio, as a system-wide service) so the menus have sound;
- mounts every disk that is not the system disk under `/media/<label>` when it is plugged in and at boot
  (`AUTOMOUNT=0` skips this; on a desktop it is skipped by itself because the desktop mounts disks;
  remove it later with `sudo sh /usr/local/lib/poniaemu/uninstall-automount`);
- sets the HDMI output level to 80% on Amlogic boards (`HDMI_VOLUME=N` changes it, `0` leaves it alone).

On a console without a desktop, `poniaemu` picks the connected screen output (HDMI first) by itself.

- Read the script first if you like: [install.sh](https://github.com/ponia2026/PoniaEmu/blob/qt6/etc/linux/install.sh).
- Install this exact version: `curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ponia2026/PoniaEmu/qt6/etc/linux/install.sh | VERSION=@TAG@ sh`
- Remove it again: `curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ponia2026/PoniaEmu/qt6/etc/linux/install.sh | sh -s -- --uninstall`
- Prefer to do it by hand? Download the matching `.tar.gz`, then `sudo tar -xzf <file> -C /usr/local`.

Requirements: an apt-based system (Debian, Ubuntu, Armbian and similar) with Qt 6.4 or newer and glibc 2.34 or newer,
for example Debian 12 or Ubuntu 24.04 and later.

On a box without a desktop the installer also helps with the **HDMI sound**: it plays short beeps (candidate N beeps N times), you tell it
which one you heard, and it makes that route the default. The choice is saved by sound card name and restored at every boot before the sound
server starts, so it survives restarts and USB sound devices coming and going. Run it again any time with `audiosetup`.

### Supported systems

Which systems each platform offers, and with which core, is listed in the [README](https://github.com/ponia2026/PoniaEmu#supported-systems).
